CHAPTER II
PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES
Article 2
Victim Protection is based on Pancasila and grounded in the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia, as well as basic principles that include:
a. non-discrimination;
b. the best interests of the Victim;
c. justice and gender equality;
d. protection of the Victim;
e. survival of the mother;
f. survival, growth, and development of the Child;
g. respect for the opinion of the Child;
h. transparency;
i. integration;
j. not blaming the Victim;
k. empowering;
l. confidentiality of the Victim; and
m. decision-making in the hands of the Victim.

CHAPTER IV
RIGHTS OF VICTIMS
Article 5
Every Victim obtains the following rights:
a. the right to have their dignity and worth as a human being respected;
b. the right to determine their own decisions;
c. the right to obtain information;
d. the right to confidentiality of their identity;
e. the right to complaint handling;
f. the right to health and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by the Victim;
g. the right to obtain facilitation in the judicial process;
h. the right to obtain repatriation efforts and social reintegration;
i. the right to social rehabilitation; and
j. the right to obtain assistance in psychological recovery, legal services and health services.

